course: everyday
lesson: 452
title: Being lied to and how it feels
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mia told her best friend Chloe a secret, and Chloe promised not to tell. By break, three people knew. When Mia asked, Chloe said «I never said anything» — but she couldn't look at her. Mia felt like the floor had dropped away, and cried in the toilets. Being lied to by a friend hurts twice: once for the secret, once for the lie. It's okay to feel angry and sad at the same time, and it's a good idea to tell a grown-up you trust.
explain: When a grown-up lets you down, it can make you feel small. Leo's dad promised to come to his football final: «Wouldn't miss it, mate.» He didn't come. He said he «got stuck at work», but Leo heard him tell Mum he'd gone to the pub. Leo felt he didn't matter. Mum talked to Dad, Dad said sorry properly, and came to every match after that. Jack trained all summer because his coach said he'd «definitely» be in the team; the coach picked someone else and said «I never promised anything.» Jack's dad said: «He shouldn't have said it. You still got fitter — that's yours.»
explain: Some lies try to make you doubt yourself. Aisha lent a friend £5 «till Friday». On Friday: «I already gave it back, don't you remember?» Aisha felt confused, then angry — she knew she hadn't got it. Her mum said, «If people make you doubt yourself, trust what you know, and write things down.» Noah «lent» his best Pokémon card to a kid who said he'd lost it; later Noah saw him trading it. Noah felt stupid — but the shame belongs to the liar, not to the person who trusted. His mum helped him ask the kid's parent, and he got it back.
explain: Lies inside families and friendships hurt most. Sam's older brother spent the birthday money Grandma posted and said «It never came, mate.» Grandma said «I definitely posted it.» Sam felt betrayed by his own brother, which was worse than losing the money. Kirsty in Inverness found out her best friend had been laughing about her in a group chat while being nice to her face. Her mum said «Aye, that's two-faced, hen» — «hen» is a Scottish way to say «love» — «spend time with folk who are the same to your face and behind your back.» Ella told Hannah she was too ill for her party; Hannah saw her at the beach on Instagram and felt «not good enough». She messaged: «You could've just told me.»
explain: Not every lie is meant to hurt. Grace's mum told her the family dog «went to a farm»; at twelve Grace found out he had died. She felt sad and a bit cheated, told her mum, and they looked at photos of him together. Oisin's friend said the teacher called Oisin «the worst in the class» — Oisin asked the teacher, who said «I said no such thing, you're doing grand.» And Liam's class pretended all day that his birthday was «tomorrow»; Liam was miserable — till the surprise cake. «You lot are horrible,» he said, crying and laughing, and hugged everyone. Even good surprises are hard while they last.
fact: Being lied to by a friend can hurt twice: once for what they did, and again for the lie.
fact: Feeling angry, sad or stupid after being lied to is normal; talking to a trusted grown-up helps.
fact: When a grown-up breaks a promise and lies about it, it is okay to tell them how it felt.
fact: Being let down by someone else is not your fault; what you learned or earned still belongs to you.
fact: Lies that make you doubt your own memory are especially unfair; writing things down helps.
fact: When someone tricks you, the shame belongs to the person who lied, not to the one who trusted.
fact: Lies from family often hurt more than lies from strangers, because we trust family most.
fact: Being nice to someone's face and unkind behind their back is called being two-faced, and it is a kind of lying.
fact: If someone tells you a hurtful thing another person «said», check with that person directly.
fact: The lie often hurts more than the truth would have; «You could've just told me» is a common feeling.
fact: Some lies are told long ago to protect a child; finding out can still be upsetting, and talking about it helps.
fact: A surprise can feel like a lie while it is happening, so a kind surprise should not drag on too long.
fact: «Hen» (Scotland) means love or dear; «aye» means yes; «folk» means people; «grand» (Ireland) means fine; «swear down» (UK slang) means «I promise»; «gutted» means very disappointed.
exercise: Why did Mia's hurt feel double? | Chloe told the secret and then lied about it | Chloe told two secrets at once | Mia had told Chloe two secrets | Chloe lied twice about the weather and the bus
exercise: What gave Chloe away? | Three people had heard it, and she couldn't look at Mia | She wrote the secret on the board for the class to see | She told the teacher herself | Mia read Chloe's diary
exercise: What did Leo hear that showed his dad's excuse was a lie? | Dad telling Mum he'd gone to the pub | Dad's boss ringing to say thanks for working late | Dad saying he forgot the match | Dad saying the car broke down
exercise: How did things get better between Leo and his dad? | Dad said sorry properly and came to every match | Leo stopped playing football | Dad bought Leo a new ball and a pair of football boots | Leo never spoke to Dad again
exercise: What did Jack's dad say still belonged to Jack? | The fitness he'd gained from training | The place in the team | The coach's promise | The trophy for the cup final at the end
exercise: What was Aisha's friend trying to make her do? | Doubt her own memory | Lend her more money next week | Forget the friendship | Pay the £5 again to be fair
exercise: What did Aisha's mum advise? | Trust what you know and write things down | Never lend anything to anyone, even a best friend | Pay the friend another £5 | Pretend it never happened
exercise: Whose shame was it when Noah's card was «lost» and then traded? | The kid who lied | Noah's, for lending it | Noah's mum's, for letting him | Nobody's, it was an accident
exercise: Why did Sam feel the brother's lie was worse than losing the money? | It came from his own brother | Grandma was very angry with everyone | He wanted to buy a game | The money was a lot
exercise: What does «two-faced» mean? | Nice to your face, unkind behind your back | Having two faces in a photo that moved | Being friends with two people | Changing your hairstyle
exercise: What does «hen» mean in Scotland? | Love or dear | A chicken only | Be quick, now | Hello, how are you
exercise: How did Hannah find out Ella wasn't ill? | Photos of the beach on Instagram | Ella's mum told her | Ella's doctor rang her mum about the beach trip | Hannah saw her at the shop
exercise: What did Hannah's message say? | «You could've just told me.» | «I'll never speak to you again.» | «I hope you get ill now.» | «I don't care at all.»
exercise: How did Oisin check what the teacher «said»? | He asked the teacher directly | He asked the friend again | He looked in his report | He asked his mam to guess what it said
exercise: How did Grace feel when she found out about the dog? | Sad and a bit cheated | Happy and very relieved | She felt nothing at all | Angry at the dog for days
exercise: How did Liam feel during the birthday trick? | Sad all day, until the surprise | Excited all day long | He knew all along and pretended to be sad | Angry at the cake
test: Mate: «I never told no-one ur secret, swear down». Three people heard it from her. What's the most likely truth? | She told it and is lying now | Three people guessed it | Mia told it herself, without thinking | Secrets spread by magic
test: Aisha: «you owe me a fiver». Friend: «I gave it back, don't you remember?» Aisha knows she didn't. What helps most? | Trust what she knows, tell a grown-up | Believe the friend, she must be right | Pay the friend another fiver | Never speak to anyone again
test: Kirsty's mum: «Aye, that's two-faced, hen.» What is Mum saying about the friend? | She's nice to Kirsty's face but not behind her back | She's got a lovely face | She's a very good friend, with a kind face | She looks like a chicken
test: Noah, gutted: «I'm so stupid for lending it.» What's true? | Trusting isn't stupid; the liar should feel ashamed | Yes, he is stupid for lending | He should never lend again, ever, because people always lie | It was his fault for having cards
test: Dad: «Wouldn't miss it, mate.» Doesn't show. «Stuck at work.» Leo knows it was the pub. What could Leo do? | Tell Dad how it made him feel | Pretend he didn't mind at all | Lie back to Dad next time | Stop playing football forever
test: «Teacher said you're the worst in the class lol.» What should Oisin do first? | Ask the teacher directly | Cry and believe it's true | Tell everyone the teacher's mean | Stop going to school for good
test: Coach: «I never PROMISED anything.» Jack is furious. What can't the coach take away? | The fitness Jack gained | Jack's football boots | Jack's team shirt | Jack's birthday
test: What would happen if Ella had told Hannah «I'm going to the beach instead, sorry»? | Hannah would be sad, but less hurt than by the lie | Hannah would be far more upset | Hannah would come to the beach with them next time | Nothing — Hannah would not care
test: Big bro: «Gran's money never came, mate.» Gran: «I posted it, love.» What's the simplest check? | Gran posted it, so ask what happened to it | Ask the postman to guess | Believe the brother because he's older and knows the post | Write to the King
test: In a cartoon, the kid who gets lied to just shrugs and is fine in one second. What's more true? | Being lied to can hurt for a while | Lies never hurt real people | Only grown-ups get hurt by lies | Cartoon kids are always right
test: Liam's class: «what, it's your birthday tomorrow, innit?» Then cake at 3 pm! Liam: «You lot are horrible.» Is he really cross? | No, he's relieved and happy | Yes, he'll never forgive them | Yes, he hates cake and surprises | No, he didn't notice the joke
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a falsehood told by a friend concerning a betrayed confidence is commonly experienced as a twofold injury. | The confidence is broken and a falsehood is then added | The friend has told two separate confidences on two occasions | The injury is experienced on two days | The friend has two faces
exam: It is given that a borrower asserts that a loan was repaid, whereas the lender is certain it was not. Select the response consistent with the established advice. | The lender should rely upon her own knowledge and inform a trusted adult | The lender should accept the borrower's assertion | The lender should repay the sum herself | The lender should conceal the matter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the shame associated with having been deceived? | It belongs properly to the deceiver and not to the person deceived | It belongs exclusively to the person deceived, who ought to have known better | It belongs equally to all persons present | It is an indication of foolishness
exam: Determine the most appropriate response to a report that a third party has made a hurtful remark about oneself. | To verify the report directly with the third party | To accept the report without inquiry | To repeat the report to others | To retaliate against the third party
